      <header class="page-header">
        <h1>Benefits of Grip Strength: Why Your Hands Matter More Than You Think</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Grip strength is associated with how long people live in large cohort studies. This page covers what grip strength is linked to, what the research does not show, and how to build and test it.</p>
      </header>
    </div>
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-meta">
        <a href="/about/" class="author-link">
          <span class="author-avatar">DH</span>
          <span>DeadHangs Team</span>
        </a>
        <span class="meta-sep">·</span>
        <span class="updated-date">Updated October 30, 2026</span>
        
      </div>
      <div class="container">

        <h2>Grip Strength and Overall Health</h2>
        <p>Handgrip strength is a widely used simple marker of overall muscle strength in clinical settings (Bohannon 2019). Clinicians read dynamometer results alongside other assessments when they track function and recovery.</p>
        <p>A 2015 Lancet study (the PURE cohort, Leong et al.) followed 139,691 adults aged 35 to 70 in 17 countries for a median of 4 years. Each 5 kg lower grip strength was associated with a 16% higher risk of death from any cause. Grip strength was associated with all-cause mortality more strongly than systolic blood pressure was.</p>
        <p>Low grip strength is used in sarcopenia screening (Bohannon 2019). Sarcopenia is the age-related loss of muscle mass and strength.</p>
        <p>These findings are associations from observational studies, and no trial shows that training grip changes mortality risk. The <a href="/longevity-research/">longevity research guide</a> sets out the evidence and its limits.</p>

        <h2>Athletic Performance</h2>
        <p>Grip is a limiting factor in many pulling movements, including deadlifts, rows and pull-ups, and climbing and grappling rely on grip endurance.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/athletic-performance/">athletic performance guide</a> covers sport-specific grip training.</p>

        <h2>Everyday Function</h2>
        <p>Opening a tight jar lid, carrying grocery bags from the car, shaking hands and turning a wrench on a stubborn bolt all depend on grip strength.</p>
        <p>Low grip strength is associated with later disability in older adults (Bohannon 2019). The tasks that get harder first are often small ones, such as opening medication bottles, buttoning shirts and gripping a handrail.</p>
        <p>Parents load their forearms every day by carrying a toddler, pushing a stroller, opening child-proof caps and assembling furniture. Painting, gardening, plumbing and carpentry tire the forearms in the same way.</p>

        <h2>Grip Strength, Falls and Injury</h2>
        <p>Researchers have linked low grip strength with falls and frailty, but trials of grip training for prevention are lacking. No trial reviewed here shows that grip training prevents elbow tendinitis or wrist fractures.</p>

        <h2>Grip Strength and Cognitive Health</h2>
        <p>Some observational studies link lower grip strength with poorer cognitive test results and a higher risk of cognitive decline. These studies show an association only, and no trial shows that grip training protects brain health.</p>

        <h2>How to Build Grip Strength</h2>
        <p>Dead hangs are the main grip training tool on this site. Hang from a bar with full bodyweight and stop a second or two before your grip gives out. Perform 3 to 4 sets with 60 to 90 seconds of rest, on 3 non-consecutive days a week. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Weekly Grip Training Plan</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Monday, Wednesday and Friday:</strong> Dead hangs, 3 to 4 sets, stopping a second or two before your grip gives out</li>
            <li><strong>Tuesday and Thursday:</strong> Supplemental work such as farmer's walks, wrist curls and plate pinches</li>
            <li><strong>Optional on any day:</strong> Rubber band finger extensions, 3 sets of 20 repetitions</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>Supplemental exercises cover grip types that dead hangs miss. Farmer's walks train support grip under movement. Plate pinches develop thumb strength. Wrist curls work the flexors through full range. Rubber band extensions work the finger extensors that oppose the flexors. The <a href="/grip-strength/">grip strength training guide</a> has detailed protocols.</p>
        <p>Grip strength can be trained at any age. Start at a level that is comfortable for you and build over months.</p>

        <h2>How to Test Your Grip Strength</h2>
        <p>Two methods give a baseline to track against your own later results. A grip dynamometer measures force in kilograms, and a timed dead hang measures endurance in seconds. The two measure different things.</p>

        <h3>Grip Dynamometer (Standard Clinical Measure)</h3>
        <p>A grip dynamometer measures peak squeeze force in kilograms. Squeeze the device with maximum effort for 3 to 5 seconds, take three attempts per hand and record the best score. In pooled data from 12 British studies with 49,964 participants, peak median grip was 51 kg in men aged 29 to 39 and 31 kg in women aged 26 to 42 (Dodds et al., PLOS ONE 2014). Force in kilograms is not hang time.</p>

        <h3>Dead Hang Time (Practical Personal Test)</h3>
        <p>Hang from a pull-up bar with an overhand grip at shoulder width and record the time from feet leaving the ground to release. No published norms exist for dead hang time. The only measured samples are on the <a href="/dead-hang-time-by-age/">dead hang time by age</a> page. Repeat the test under the same conditions and compare your own results.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Why is grip strength important for health?</h3>
          <p>Grip strength is associated with lower mortality risk in large cohort studies, including the PURE study of 139,691 adults aged 35 to 70 (Leong et al., Lancet 2015). Handgrip strength is also a widely used simple marker of overall muscle strength in clinical settings (Bohannon 2019). These are associations, and no trial shows that raising grip strength changes health outcomes.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How do I test my grip strength at home?</h3>
          <p>Time a dead hang and repeat it under the same conditions. Dead hang time is a rough personal measure, not a validated test, and no published norms exist for it. A hand dynamometer is the standard clinical measure.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Does grip strength affect brain health?</h3>
          <p>Some observational studies link lower grip strength with poorer cognitive test results and a higher risk of cognitive decline. These studies show an association only, and no trial shows that grip training protects brain health.</p>
        </div>

